      "prose_summary": "The story is narrated by a young girl of twelve who spends time in a village in north Karnataka with her grandparents. Set in an era of poor transport and delayed newspapers, the household eagerly awaits the weekly Karmaveera magazine, which carries a serialised Kannada novel, Kashi Yatre, by the beloved writer Triveni. The novel tells of an old woman who sacrifices her life savings to fund an orphan girl's wedding rather than fulfil her own dream of visiting Kashi — a choice that moves readers deeply. The narrator's grandmother, Krishtakka (called Avva, meaning 'mother' in north Karnataka Kannada), is illiterate and depends entirely on the narrator to read each episode aloud. She listens with total concentration and discusses the story with friends at the temple courtyard, deeply identifying with the protagonist since she, too, never went to Kashi.\n\nWhen the narrator goes to a cousin's wedding in a neighbouring village and overstays for a week, she returns to find her grandmother in tears — unprecedented for a woman who has faced life's hardships stoically. In a tender midnight conversation on the open terrace, Krishtakka reveals the cause: the magazine arrived while the narrator was away, and she could not read the next episode. She felt helpless and ashamed to ask neighbours for help, and the experience exposed her painful dependence despite the family's financial comfort.\n\nKrishtakka declares a bold resolution: she will learn the Kannada alphabet, use Saraswati Puja day during Dassara as her deadline, and read a novel on her own. The narrator initially laughs at the idea of her grey-haired, wrinkled sixty-two-year-old grandmother going back to basics, but Krishtakka responds with quiet dignity — 'For a good cause if you are determined, you can overcome any obstacle. For learning there is no age bar.' Tuition begins the next morning. Krishtakka proves to be a diligent student: she reads, repeats, writes, and recites. By the Dassara festival she meets her deadline. In a deeply symbolic gesture, the grandmother touches the narrator's feet — reversing the traditional elder–younger hierarchy — explaining that she is honouring not her granddaughter but her teacher. The narrator gives her gift: a copy of Kashi Yatre, now published as a novel. Krishtakka reads the title and the publisher's name immediately, confirming she has 'passed with flying colours'. The story, authored by Sudha Murty, celebrates literacy, determination, and the transformative power of intergenerational love.",
