comed on every threshold — he reads letters aloud and drafts replies in his shaky hand. He receives small gestures of warmth: a cup of tea from the BSF camp, a piece of jaggery on news of births or weddings. The one delivery he dreads is the envelope with its right corner torn off — the sign of a death notice (Ashubh Samachar) — which he reads aloud twice outside the house and then tears to bits, believing bad news must be destroyed. The piece closes with Khetaram waiting optimistically for phone lines to reach his territory, when he hopes to become a Gramin Sanchar Sewak, carrying both a cell phone and the post. His words — 'Main tayyar hoon' (I'm ready) — capture his enduring spirit. The author pays tribute to people like Khetaram as integral threads in India's social fabric.",
